A wafer fab uses up four families of inputs:
- photoresists and their monomer/photo-acid-generator building blocks, used in lithography;
- ultra-pure wet chemicals for cleaning and etching, such as hydrogen peroxide, isopropyl alcohol, ammonia water, sulfuric acid and hydrofluoric acid;
- electronic specialty gases, such as NF3, CF4, SF6, neon and arsine;
- bulk high-purity gases (nitrogen, oxygen, argon).
The Sumitomo-Tata Electronics MoU names exactly this basket for the Dholera fab: photoresists, functional chemicals, and high-purity H2O2, IPA, ammonia water and sulfuric acid (S6). Acutaas describes its Korean JV's products as ultra-high-purity semiconductor materials, with impurities measured in parts per billion (S7).
